\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The base and its retained-law fibers}
\date{2026-11-08}

\p{Fix an incidence presentation #{\Pi=(V,E,s,h)}, with #{|V|\ge2}.
Its parameter set and selector set are
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Par}_{\Pi}&=\mathbb R_{>0}^E\times\mathbb R_{>0},\\
\operatorname{Sel}_V&=\{P:P^2=P=P^{\dagger},\ 0<\operatorname{rank}P<|V|\},\\
\mathcal B_\Pi&=\operatorname{Par}_\Pi\times\operatorname{Sel}_V.
\end{aligned}}
The counting metric is fixed. Each rank component of
#{\operatorname{Sel}_V} identifies with the real Grassmannian
#{\operatorname{Gr}_r(H)}, the set of #{r}-dimensional subspaces
of #{H}, by
#{P\mapsto\operatorname{im}P}; the unique orthogonal projector gives
the inverse identification. No topology or smooth structure is needed
for the following groupoid description.}

\p{Only the directed automorphism group #{\operatorname{Aut}(\Pi)}
acts on this fixed-presentation set. For #{g=(p,q)}, let
#{U_g\delta_v=\delta_{p(v)}} and put
##{g(w,m,P)=(w\circ q^{-1},m,U_gPU_g^{-1}).}
Its action groupoid #{\operatorname{Aut}(\Pi)\ltimes\mathcal B_\Pi}
is the full subgroupoid of #{\mathsf{Bg}} on backgrounds whose
presentation is exactly #{\Pi}: in both cases arrows are precisely
the same graph automorphisms satisfying the parameter equations.
Replacing directed automorphisms by signed endpoint automorphisms
gives the corresponding full subgroupoid of #{\mathsf{Bg}^{\pm}}.}

\p{To allow arrows between presentations, take
#{\mathcal B=\coprod_\Pi\mathcal B_\Pi}, over a fixed set of
finite labelled presentations, with #{\mathsf{Bg}} as its groupoid
of arrows. For example one may use finite subsets of a fixed
countable universe as carriers; every finite presentation has an
isomorphic labelled representative. There is no single action of
#{\operatorname{Aut}(\Pi)} on all presentations.}

\p{Write #{h_H} for the counting inner product on #{H=\mathbb R^V}.
Let #{\mathcal S} consist of triples #{(H,h_H,P)} on these vertex
spaces with #{P\in\operatorname{Sel}_V}, and let #{\mathcal D}
consist of quadruples #{(H,h_H,Q,P)} with the same data and
#{Q=Q^{\dagger}>0}. The \newvocab{raw selector map} and the
\newvocab{sufficient Gaussian data map} are
##{\begin{aligned}
s_{\mathrm{sel}}:\mathcal B&\longrightarrow\mathcal S,
&s_{\mathrm{sel}}(b)&=(H,h_H,P),\\
j:\mathcal B&\longrightarrow\mathcal D,
&j(b)&=(H,h_H,\extd^\dagger W\extd+m^2\bbI,P).
\end{aligned}}
The map #{s_{\mathrm{sel}}} extracts the supplied projector; it does not choose one
from the incidence data. Both maps forget incidence. The metric
specifies the adjoints and the induced Euclidean volumes, so there
is no independent volume choice in these tuples.}

\p{The \newvocab{selected-structure map} is
##{\sigma:\mathcal B\longrightarrow\operatorname{Ob}(\mathcal Q),\qquad
\sigma(b)=(X,S_P),\quad X=\operatorname{im}P,}
where #{\mathcal Q} is the groupoid of positive-dimensional real
Euclidean spaces with positive self-adjoint precisions and isometric
precision-intertwining arrows. More precisely, the displayed
object map extends to a functor #{\mathsf{Bg}\to\mathcal Q}, or
#{\mathsf{Bg}^{\pm}\to\mathcal Q}, by sending an arrow to #{U|_X}.
The \vocabk{transport identities}{fdip-005C} and ordinary restriction of
composites prove functoriality. The symbol #{\sigma} denotes this
assignment, whereas #{P:H\to H} is the field-space projector.}

\p{In terms of the sufficient data, this is #{\sigma=\kappa\circ j},
where
##{\begin{aligned}
\kappa:\mathcal D&\longrightarrow\operatorname{Ob}(\mathcal Q),\\
\kappa(H,h_H,Q,P)&=(X,C_P^{-1}),\\
X&=\operatorname{im}P,\qquad C_P=(PQ^{-1}P)|_X.
\end{aligned}}
The image carries the restricted metric. The positivity and inverse
in this formula are supplied by \ref{fdip-005B}. Thus the output is
an operator on its specified Euclidean space, including when the
dimension varies.}

\p{The retained-law fiber over #{b} is the probability space
##{\mathcal F_b=(X,\mathcal B(X),\nu_b),\qquad
\nu_b=N_X(0,S_P^{-1}).}
Here #{\mathcal B(X)} is the Borel sigma-algebra. Arrows transport
these fibers by measure-preserving isometries and transport
observables by pullback. Thus a fiber is a specified law on a
specified space, rather than a bare covariance matrix.
If an interaction is additionally supplied, its induced retained
vertices require that further data; they are not part of the present
Gaussian tuple.}

\p{Gaussian induction assigns to #{(X,S)\in\operatorname{Ob}(\mathcal Q)}
the probability space #{\iota(X,S)=(X,\mathcal B(X),N_X(0,S^{-1}))},
using the Euclidean volume of #{X}. Thus
#{\mathcal F_b=(\iota\circ\kappa\circ j)(b)}.
For an arrow with selected isometry
#{U_X:X\to X'}, observable pullback is
##{U_X^*:L^1(X',\nu_{b'})\longrightarrow L^1(X,\nu_b),
\qquad U_X^*F'=F'\circ U_X.}
Here #{L^1} denotes actual integrable real Borel functions, as in
\ref{fdip-000E}. Integrability is preserved by the measure transport
for \vocabk{background arrows}{fdip-005C}.
Successive selected isometries
#{U_X:X\to X'} and #{V_X:X'\to X''} obey
#{(V_XU_X)^*=U_X^*V_X^*}; the identity pulls back to the identity.
For a measure-preserving left configuration action #{a_g} on #{X},
this convention gives the right observable action
#{a_g^{\dagger}a_h^{\dagger}=a_{hg}^{\dagger}}.
The projection #{P:H\to X} and these isometries are continuous,
hence measurable for the Borel sigma-algebras.}

\p{Forgetting #{P} gives the unselected weighted incidence data
#{(\Pi,w,m)} and their full law #{\mu_Q}. It does not by itself
give a \vocabk{coordinate background}{fdip-000G}, which also requires a
retained subset #{R}. Choosing #{P=P_R} recovers that background
and its [Schur marginal](fdip-0018). Equivalence here means
isomorphism in the chosen graph groupoid; the
\vocabk{orthogonal comparison}{fdip-005D} is coarser. For a different
operation, the supplied cycle scaling of the
\vocabk{circle comparison}{fdip-004H} is a limit of models, not a graph arrow.}
